item.text:
**Reused evidence**
None from the earlier chapter dossiers applies cleanly to Chapter 6B for Bangladesh in 2022.

**New evidence**
I screened about 30 documents and kept 18 claim units across 11 URLs, prioritizing direct welfare, crisis-response, distributional, and implementation evidence.

- IMF macro baseline and social-development trajectory.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Concludes 2021 Article IV Consultation with Bangladesh","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-03-03","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/03/03/pr2257-imf-executive-board-concludes-2021-article-iv-consultation-with-bangladesh","claim":"Since 2010, per capita real GDP growth averaging 5 percent annually had produced a steady poverty decline and rising access to education and healthcare, while macroeconomic policies kept inflation stable, debt-to-GDP low, and external buffers adequate.","locator":"L240","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official IMF assessment with specific macro and welfare claims, directly tied to Bangladesh's pre-2022 policy environment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly frames the inherited baseline and the 2022 policy environment.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly attributable to the Hasina government and its incumbent policy regime, though the source is macro-level and not a personal decision log.","contrary_evidence":["Does not isolate Sheikh Hasina's personal role in every underlying reform or distributional outcome."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.10"]}

- IMF pandemic response and fiscal reprioritization.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Concludes 2021 Article IV Consultation with Bangladesh","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-03-03","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/03/03/pr2257-imf-executive-board-concludes-2021-article-iv-consultation-with-bangladesh","claim":"The authorities reacted quickly and decisively to the pandemic by announcing support packages worth Tk 1.9 trillion, financed partly by curtailing non-priority current spending and suspending low-priority capital projects, while vaccination was catching up.","locator":"L241","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specific IMF press-release claim with clear fiscal and crisis-response detail and strong period fit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Describes the government's immediate post-pandemic response relevant to 2022 conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the incumbent government, not exclusively to Hasina personally.","contrary_evidence":["The source is descriptive rather than an independent audit of implementation quality."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.9"]}

- IMF fiscal prudence and reform direction.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Concludes 2021 Article IV Consultation with Bangladesh","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-03-03","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/03/03/pr2257-imf-executive-board-concludes-2021-article-iv-consultation-with-bangladesh","claim":"IMF directors commended fiscal prudence and low debt distress, but urged revenue and tax reform, stronger banking regulation and corporate governance, and more effective social, developmental, and climate spending.","locator":"L249-L250","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Strong official source with specific policy prescriptions and explicit praise/cautions on fiscal and social spending management.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant to the 2022 fiscal and social-policy environment.","ruler_attribution":"Primarily attributable to the Hasina government and its policy apparatus, though not to a single official decision.","contrary_evidence":["The passage also signals unresolved banking and revenue weaknesses."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.9"]}

- IMF climate-resilience reform agenda.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Staff Statement on Bangladesh","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-10-21","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/News/Articles/2022/10/21/pr22360-bangladesh-imf-staff-statement","claim":"IMF staff planned talks with Bangladeshi authorities on economic and financial reforms and policies, including a prospective RSF arrangement intended to provide long-term financing for resilience against climate risks.","locator":"L226-L227","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official IMF statement with explicit reform and resilience financing language, directly time-stamped in 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year and tied to the government's reform agenda.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the incumbent government as the negotiating counterpart and policy sponsor.","contrary_evidence":["The statement announces discussions, not completed outcomes."],"lenses":["6B.5","6B.6","6B.9"]}

- World Bank climate resilience and disaster preparedness.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Key Highlights: Country Climate and Development Report for Bangladesh","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-10-31","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/feature/2022/10/31/key-highlights-country-climate-and-development-report-for-bangladesh","claim":"Bangladesh's growth was backed by decades of systematic investments in climate resilience and disaster preparedness; the country had reduced cyclone-related deaths by 100-fold since 1970, but the poorest would be hardest hit without further action.","locator":"L56 and L156-L157","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank feature story with concrete, Bangladesh-specific claims and explicit distributional consequence language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Fits the 2022 climate-and-social-risk environment affecting household welfare.","ruler_attribution":"This is partly inherited/system-level credit rather than a discrete Hasina-only act, but it reflects the governing state's welfare and resilience choices.","contrary_evidence":["It also warns that Bangladesh still faces severe and increasing climate risks."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- World Bank education access for disadvantaged children.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Bank Helps Give Bangladesh's Poor Children a Chance at Education","publisher":"World Bank Group","publication_date":"2022-10-26","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/results/2022/10/26/world-bank-helps-give-bangladesh-s-poor-children-a-chance-at-education","claim":"The ROSC II project provided primary education to 735,000 disadvantaged children, half of them girls, from underserved rural areas and urban slums, and supported 112,000 displaced Rohingya children who fled conflict in Myanmar.","locator":"L68 and L81-L82","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct World Bank results page with concrete beneficiary counts and distributional detail.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Strongly relevant to 2022 education access and vulnerable-group support.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to state policy direction and program implementation under the Hasina government, though delivery also depended on long-running project machinery.","contrary_evidence":["The challenge section notes that millions of children still remained out of school, so the gain is partial rather than universal."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- World Bank education quality, teacher training, and outcomes.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Bank Helps Give Bangladesh's Poor Children a Chance at Education","publisher":"World Bank Group","publication_date":"2022-10-26","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/results/2022/10/26/world-bank-helps-give-bangladesh-s-poor-children-a-chance-at-education","claim":"ROSC II trained 18,000 rural learning-center teachers and 3,000 Cox's Bazar teachers; average grade retention reached 83 percent and the completion-exam pass rate reached 84 percent.","locator":"L81-L83","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Concrete implementation and outcome metrics from a World Bank results brief with clear period fit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Relevant to the 2022 education-service delivery environment and program maturity.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the government's program management and donor-supported delivery system, not just to the ruler personally.","contrary_evidence":["These are project-level results, not a nationwide education-system audit."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.9"]}

- UNICEF child benefit scaling and maternity support.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Public Finance for Children","publisher":"UNICEF Bangladesh","publication_date":"2023-05","url":"https://www.unicef.org/bangladesh/en/reports/public-finance-children","claim":"Bangladesh's flagship Mother and Child Benefit Programme covered 1.25 million beneficiaries in 2022-23 and provided a monthly benefit of BDT 800 to enrolled pregnant women and mothers.","locator":"L61-L66","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF Bangladesh policy note with specific program design and coverage figures, directly tied to 2022-23.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the target period's social-protection design and scale.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Hasina government's social-protection policy choices, though the page is a UNICEF policy note rather than a government accounting document.","contrary_evidence":["The page also notes implementation challenges and recommends benefit-value adjustment for inflation."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.9"]}

- UNICEF child-budget execution weaknesses.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Public Finance for Children","publisher":"UNICEF Bangladesh","publication_date":"2023-05","url":"https://www.unicef.org/bangladesh/en/reports/public-finance-children","claim":"The child budget share remained stagnant at 15.05 percent of the national budget in FY21-22, and UNICEF identified low utilisation of allocated resources and rushed end-of-year spending as implementation challenges.","locator":"L67-L71","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF policy note identifies both allocative and implementation problems with concrete percentages and a clear diagnosis.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly informative for 2022 implementation quality even though published in 2023.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the government's service-delivery apparatus rather than to a single person; it is a real implementation critique, not just a budget claim.","contrary_evidence":["The note also says expanded social protection for children is recommended, implying current practice was not yet sufficient."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.9"]}

- Budget speech on welfare-oriented priorities.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Budget Speech","publisher":"The Financial Express","publication_date":"2022-06-10","url":"https://today.thefinancialexpress.com.bd/budget-2022-23/budget-speech-1654804655?amp=true","claim":"The FY2022-23 budget was framed around recovery from COVID-19 and the Russia-Ukraine war, with expansion of social safety nets, education and training, job creation, agricultural subsidies, and sustaining economic development.","locator":"search-result snippet lines 54-56","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"A full transcript of the budget speech as republished by a reputable local outlet, with specific policy priorities and date fit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about the 2022-23 budget that shaped 2022 social policy.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Hasina government and budget leadership; the speech is policy intent rather than independent outcome verification.","contrary_evidence":["Intent alone does not prove delivery or equitable impact."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.3","6B.8"]}

- Budget speech on safety-net scale and transfer design.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Budget Speech","publisher":"The Financial Express","publication_date":"2022-06-10","url":"https://today.thefinancialexpress.com.bd/budget-2022-23/budget-speech-1654804655?amp=true","claim":"The budget set social safety-net spending at Tk 113,576 crore, or 16.75 percent of the budget and 2.55 percent of GDP, expanded G2P cash transfers to 26.3 million beneficiaries, and said roughly five crore low-income people would directly benefit from the initiative.","locator":"search-result snippet lines 160 and 162","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Specific numerical budget claim from a direct speech transcript, though accessed via a media republication rather than an official host page.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly tied to the 2022-23 fiscal year and its 2022 implementation window.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Hasina government; it reflects allocative choices but not detailed district-level implementation.","contrary_evidence":["Large allocations do not by themselves prove effective delivery or unbiased targeting."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.7","6B.10"]}

- Budget speech on inclusion of marginalized groups.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Budget Speech","publisher":"The Financial Express","publication_date":"2022-06-10","url":"https://today.thefinancialexpress.com.bd/budget-2022-23/budget-speech-1654804655?amp=true","claim":"The government said it was rehabilitating beggars, creating alternative employment, improving living standards for tea garden workers, nomadic Bedes and transgender communities, and providing stipends to students from Bede and marginalized communities.","locator":"search-result snippet lines 159-162","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Directly quoted policy speech text with explicit references to vulnerable groups and targeted programs.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant to 2022 social-protection and inclusion policy.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Hasina government and its social-policy agenda.","contrary_evidence":["The speech alone does not establish how broad or durable the benefits were in practice."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- School WASH deficits.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Schools ill-equipped to provide healthy and inclusive learning environments for all children - UNICEF, WHO","publisher":"UNICEF Bangladesh","publication_date":"2022-06-23","url":"https://www.unicef.org/bangladesh/en/press-releases/schools-ill-equipped-provide-healthy-and-inclusive-learning-environments-all","claim":"In Bangladesh, 19 percent of schools lacked safe drinking water, 43 percent lacked basic sanitation with privacy, 44 percent lacked basic hygiene services, and 7 percent had no WASH facilities at all.","locator":"L66-L67","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Joint UNICEF-WHO press release with concrete Bangladesh-specific infrastructure deficits and precise percentages.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and directly about child-facing service conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the state service environment under Hasina, though school WASH is partly shared with local administration and legacy infrastructure.","contrary_evidence":["The report describes a broader global WASH problem, so Bangladesh is not uniquely singled out on governance grounds."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- UNICEF 2022 child-health outcomes.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Child-Related SDG Progress Assessment for Bangladesh","publisher":"UNICEF DATA","publication_date":"unknown","url":"https://data.unicef.org/sdgs/country/bgd/","claim":"For Bangladesh, the 2022 child-related SDG dashboard listed stunting under age 5 at 26 percent, MCV1 at 97 percent, DTP3 at 98 percent, under-five mortality at 27 per 1,000 live births, neonatal mortality at 16 per 1,000, and skilled-birth attendance at 59 percent.","locator":"L4943-L4945","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF's official data dashboard with explicit 2022 values and clear indicator definitions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly reports 2022 values for the target year.","ruler_attribution":"These are country-level outcomes under the incumbent government, but they do not isolate Hasina's personal causal contribution.","contrary_evidence":["The same dashboard shows high remaining gaps in stunting and maternal/newborn care."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.5","6B.10"]}

- UNICEF 2022 water, sanitation, poverty, and social-protection indicators.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Child-Related SDG Progress Assessment for Bangladesh","publisher":"UNICEF DATA","publication_date":"unknown","url":"https://data.unicef.org/sdgs/country/bgd/","claim":"The 2022 Bangladesh dashboard showed basic drinking-water access at 98 percent, basic sanitation at 59 percent, safely managed sanitation at 31 percent, basic handwashing at 62 percent, child extreme poverty at 5 percent, and child social-protection coverage at 46 percent.","locator":"L5049-L5052 and L5063-L5063","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UNICEF dashboard with explicit country-year indicators and a transparent methodology note.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly reports 2022 values for the target year, with the social-protection indicator drawn from the same dashboard.","ruler_attribution":"Country-level outcomes reflect the Hasina-era welfare state and constraints, but not a single ruler action.","contrary_evidence":["Safe-water and sanitation coverage remain incomplete, so the record is mixed rather than near-universal."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- Flood emergency response.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Millions of children in flood-affected Bangladesh in need of food, clean water and protection","publisher":"UNICEF Bangladesh","publication_date":"2022-07-17","url":"https://www.unicef.org/bangladesh/en/stories/millions-children-flood-affected-bangladesh-need-food-clean-water-and-protection","claim":"The July 2022 floods disrupted the lives of 3.5 million children, damaged about 45,000 water points and 50,000 sanitation facilities, and UNICEF said it had delivered over a million water purification tablets, along with other emergency supplies, to support the government's response.","locator":"L77-L77 and L99-L104","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct UNICEF field report with concrete emergency-response numbers and clear 2022 timing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in the target year and directly about crisis mitigation.","ruler_attribution":"The response was government-linked and state-coordinated, though UNICEF also provided major external support.","contrary_evidence":["The article also shows how severe the flood damage was, so the crisis response was compensatory rather than preventive."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.7","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- WFP food-security and malnutrition baseline.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh country strategic plan (2022-2026)","publisher":"World Food Programme","publication_date":"2021-11","url":"https://www.wfp.org/operations/bd02-bangladesh-country-strategic-plan-2022-2026","claim":"The country strategic plan says Bangladesh is self-sufficient in rice, meat and fish and on track to reduced stunting and wasting, but still faces a triple burden of malnutrition, persistent gender inequality, and a continued need for shock-responsive social protection; it also notes that 96 percent of Rohingya refugees would be unable to meet basic needs without assistance.","locator":"L264-L268","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official WFP country strategy with explicit Bangladesh-specific welfare and vulnerability diagnostics.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly frames the 2022-2026 welfare environment and inherited baseline.","ruler_attribution":"This is more baseline/context than ruler-specific credit, but it reflects the state welfare environment under Hasina's government.","contrary_evidence":["The strategy emphasizes persistent malnutrition and inequality, so the social picture remains incomplete."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- World Bank poverty and living-conditions improvement.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Structural Reforms are Essential to Sustain Growth & Poverty Reduction in Bangladesh","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2023-10-05","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/feature/2023/10/05/structural-reforms-are-essential-to-sustain-growth-poverty-reduction-in-bangladesh","claim":"The World Bank said extreme poverty fell to 5.0 percent in 2022 from 9.0 percent in 2016, and that other wellbeing measures improved, including infant mortality, stunting, and access to electricity, sanitary toilets, and education; rural areas saw faster poverty reduction than urban areas.","locator":"L35-L37","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Strong World Bank feature story explicitly keyed to 2022 household survey data and distributional changes.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective but directly about 2022 outcomes and household survey data.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the broader Hasina-era policy environment, not exclusively to 2022 executive action.","contrary_evidence":["The same source notes rising inflation and financial vulnerabilities, and that urban inequality widened."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- IMF social-protection implementation, digital targeting, and transparency.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh: 2021 Article IV Consultation-Press Release; Staff Report; and Statement by the Executive Director for Bangladesh","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-03-07","url":"https://www.elibrary.imf.org/view/journals/002/2022/071/article-A001-en.xml","claim":"Social-protection spending was budgeted at 3.2 percent of GDP in FY22; expanded programs included widow and old-age allowances, a new urban-poor database, and Tk 27 billion in cash transfers estimated to benefit around 9 million people, with G2P payments and mobile financial services used to improve transparency and reach unbanked beneficiaries.","locator":"search-result snippet / social-protection summary","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official IMF staff report with very specific program details; the full text is blocked here, but the excerpt is detailed and corroborated by other sources.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly concerns FY22 social-protection implementation in the target period.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Hasina government and Bangladesh's social-protection administration, though not a personal decision record.","contrary_evidence":["The full-text page was blocked in this environment, so this claim relies on the accessible IMF summary excerpt."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.7","6B.9"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I screened roughly 30 documents across IMF, World Bank, UNICEF, WHO, WFP, Bangladesh government/parliament budget pages, local media, and retrospective poverty/education sources.
- I rejected repeated coverage of the same budget speech when a cleaner or more specific excerpt already existed, and I did not duplicate the same claim across multiple republished articles.
- I rejected generic country pages and dashboards when they added no Bangladesh-2022 claim beyond the more specific UNICEF or World Bank pages.
- I treated 2025-style dashboard pages and 2024-2026 updates as context only unless they explicitly reported 2022 results from household surveys or country-year indicators.
- I discarded blocked or inaccessible full texts when their accessible summaries already carried the needed claim: the WFP annual-country-report PDF download and the IMF eLibrary full text both returned access errors, so I relied on accessible snippets and corroborating pages instead.
- I treated Reuters inflation coverage and similar macro-news items as redundant once the IMF and World Bank sources had already captured the inflation, reserve, and policy-tightening story more precisely.
- I treated the UNICEF and WFP 2022-2026 strategy pages as useful mainly for baseline, inherited conditions, and durability, not as proof of single-year Hasina personal causation.

**Remaining gaps**
- Direct ruler-attributed evidence on ministerial or bureaucratic competence in health, education, and social protection is thinner than the outcome evidence.
- The record still has limited direct proof about whether welfare delivery was systematically depoliticized, beyond the suggestive G2P and digital-targeting evidence.
- More search would help on district-level service quality, budget execution versus announcement, and independent evaluations of 2022 program implementation in Bangladesh.
- No score assigned, as requested.
